<section id="tips" class="section"><div class="card"><h2>How to write an 8-mark answer</h2><ol><li>Start with a short introduction.</li><li>Write clear headings and numbered points.</li><li>Explain every point in simple sentences.</li><li>Add one example.</li><li>Draw a flowchart if suitable.</li><li>Finish with a conclusion.</li></ol></div></section>
<section id="five" class="section"><h2>5-Mark Questions and Answers</h2>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 1</span><h3>List three distinguished categories of multi-factor authentication.</h3><ol><li><b>Something you know:</b> Password, PIN or security answer.</li><li><b>Something you have:</b> Phone, OTP token or smart card.</li><li><b>Something you are:</b> Fingerprint, face or iris.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A bank app asks for a password and an OTP sent to the user's phone.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 2</span><h3>List the methods used to prevent authentication abuse.</h3><ol><li>Use strong and unique passwords.</li><li>Enable multi-factor authentication.</li><li>Lock an account temporarily after repeated failed attempts.</li><li>Use CAPTCHA and rate limiting.</li><li>Monitor unusual login locations and devices.</li><li>Use secure password reset and session management.</li></ol></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 3</span><h3>Explain the importance of protecting user accounts.</h3><ol><li>Protects private messages, photos and personal details.</li><li>Prevents financial loss and unauthorised payments.</li><li>Stops identity theft and misuse.</li><li>Protects files from theft or deletion.</li><li>Builds trust in online services.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> MFA can stop an attacker even if the password is stolen.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 4</span><h3>Explain the factors considered in account reputation scoring.</h3><ol><li>Account age and history.</li><li>Usual login locations and devices.</li><li>Number of failed login attempts.</li><li>Unusual transactions or activity.</li><li>Past fraud reports or suspicious behaviour.</li><li>Whether current activity matches normal behaviour.</li></ol></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 5</span><h3>Explain the types of biometric authentication.</h3><ol><li>Fingerprint recognition.</li><li>Face recognition.</li><li>Iris recognition.</li><li>Voice recognition.</li><li>Keystroke recognition.</li><li>Signature recognition.</li></ol></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 6</span><h3>How are typing patterns used to verify a user's identity?</h3><ol><li>Record the user's typing during enrolment.</li><li>Measure how long each key is pressed (dwell time).</li><li>Measure the time between key events (flight time).</li><li>Create and save a typing profile.</li><li>Compare the typing pattern during the next login.</li><li>Accept the user if the pattern matches sufficiently; otherwise ask for extra verification.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> Two people can type the same password but have different typing speed and rhythm.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 7</span><h3>Explain the steps involved in analysing fraud data using Jupyter Notebook.</h3><ol><li>Open Jupyter Notebook and create a Python notebook.</li><li>Import Pandas, NumPy and Matplotlib.</li><li>Load the fraud dataset.</li><li>Clean missing values, duplicates and errors.</li><li>Explore data using tables, statistics and charts.</li><li>Prepare features and train an ML model.</li><li>Test the model and check its results.</li></ol></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 8</span><h3>Analyse the role of machine-learning algorithms in detecting fraudulent activities.</h3><ol><li>Learn patterns from previous genuine and fraud transactions.</li><li>Automatically check new transactions.</li><li>Find unusual amount, location, time or spending behaviour.</li><li>Classify activity as normal or suspicious.</li><li>Find patterns that are difficult to notice manually.</li><li>Help teams respond quickly; monitor false alarms.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A card normally used in one town suddenly makes a large purchase in another country. The model may flag it.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • 9</span><h3>Explain the effectiveness of boosting techniques.</h3><ol><li>Combines many weak models into a stronger model.</li><li>Models are trained one after another.</li><li>Each new model focuses on earlier errors.</li><li>Can improve prediction accuracy.</li><li>Useful in fraud detection and classification.</li><li>May overfit or take longer if poorly tuned.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> AdaBoost and Gradient Boosting improve predictions by learning from earlier model errors.</div></div>
</section>
<section id="eight" class="section"><h2>8-Mark Questions — Full Answers + Malayalam</h2>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 1</span><h3>Analyse the role of strong passwords, account lockout and multi-factor authentication in preventing authentication abuse.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Authentication abuse is an attempt to enter an account without permission. Three important protections are strong passwords, account lockout and MFA.</p><h4>1. Strong passwords</h4><ul><li>Use long passwords with letters, numbers and symbols.</li><li>Use a different password for each account.</li><li>Avoid names, birthdays and common words.</li><li>A password manager can create and store unique passwords.</li></ul><h4>2. Account lockout</h4><ul><li>The system counts failed login attempts.</li><li>After a set number of failures, it temporarily locks the account or slows attempts.</li><li>This makes repeated password guessing difficult.</li><li>Safe account recovery must be provided to genuine users.</li></ul><h4>3. Multi-factor authentication</h4><ul><li>Asks for two or more different proofs.</li><li>Examples: password, OTP/security key and fingerprint.</li><li>If a password is stolen, the attacker may not have the second factor.</li></ul><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A bank app asks for a password and OTP. Repeated wrong passwords temporarily lock the account. Together these controls make unauthorised access harder.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Using all three methods gives better protection than using only one.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 2</span><h3>Explain different types of authentication abuse and their effects.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Authentication abuse means using dishonest or illegal methods to access a user's account.</p><h4>Types</h4><ol><li><b>Brute-force:</b> Tries many password combinations.</li><li><b>Credential stuffing:</b> Uses stolen username/password pairs from other websites.</li><li><b>Password spraying:</b> Tries a few common passwords against many accounts.</li><li><b>Phishing:</b> Tricks users into giving passwords or OTPs through fake messages or pages.</li><li><b>Session hijacking:</b> Steals or misuses a valid login session.</li><li><b>Dictionary attack:</b> Tries passwords from a prepared word list.</li></ol><h4>Effects</h4><ul><li>Loss of private data and files.</li><li>Financial loss and unauthorised payments.</li><li>Identity theft and misuse of accounts.</li><li>Damage to an organisation's reputation.</li><li>Loss of trust and possible service disruption.</li></ul><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A fake bank message asks for login details. The attacker uses them to enter the real bank account. This is phishing and may cause money loss.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> MFA, strong passwords, user awareness and login monitoring reduce these attacks.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 3</span><h3>Explain Account Reputation Scoring.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Account Reputation Scoring estimates how trustworthy or risky an account is by studying its history and behaviour.</p><h4>Factors</h4><ol><li>Account age and history.</li><li>Usual login locations, times and devices.</li><li>Repeated failed login attempts.</li><li>Unusual payments or transactions.</li><li>Past fraud reports or suspicious activity.</li><li>Whether new activity matches normal behaviour.</li></ol><h4>Working steps</h4><div class="flow"><span>Collect account data</span>→<span>Study behaviour</span>→<span>Calculate score</span>→<span>Decide risk</span></div><ul><li>The system collects account activity.</li><li>It compares the activity with normal patterns.</li><li>It calculates a score or risk level.</li><li>High-risk activity may require OTP, extra checks or manual review.</li></ul><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> An account usually logs in from one phone and makes small payments. Suddenly it logs in from a new country and tries a large payment. The system may mark it as high risk.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> It helps identify suspicious accounts early and should be combined with other security checks.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 4</span><h3>Explain the steps involved in Keystroke Recognition.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Keystroke recognition is a behavioural biometric method that identifies a person by the way they type.</p><ol><li><b>Enrolment:</b> User types a given text several times.</li><li><b>Data collection:</b> Record key press and release times.</li><li><b>Feature extraction:</b> Measure dwell time, flight time, speed and rhythm.</li><li><b>Profile creation:</b> Save the normal typing pattern.</li><li><b>Verification:</b> Record the typing pattern during a later login.</li><li><b>Comparison:</b> Compare the new pattern with the saved profile.</li><li><b>Decision:</b> Accept if sufficiently similar; otherwise ask for extra verification.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A user may type a password quickly but pause before certain letters. The system learns this timing pattern.</div><p><b>Advantages:</b> It may need no extra hardware and can support continuous checking. <b>Limitation:</b> Tiredness, injury or a different keyboard can change typing behaviour.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> It adds identity checking by studying typing behaviour.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 5</span><h3>Identify and explain the types of Keystroke features.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Keystroke features are measurements taken from typing behaviour to create a user's typing profile.</p><ol><li><b>Dwell time:</b> How long a key is held down.</li><li><b>Flight time:</b> Time between key events, commonly releasing one key and pressing the next.</li><li><b>Typing speed:</b> Number of characters or words typed in a given time.</li><li><b>Typing rhythm:</b> Repeated timing pattern while typing.</li><li><b>Key transition time:</b> Time taken to move from one key to another.</li><li><b>Error pattern:</b> Common typing mistakes, backspaces and corrections.</li><li><b>Pause duration:</b> Time gaps between words or groups of keys.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> One user may hold a key longer and quickly press the next key. Another may type with a different rhythm.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Combining several features creates a better profile than using typing speed alone.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 6</span><h3>Explain the steps involved in Facial Recognition.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Facial recognition identifies or verifies a person by analysing facial features.</p><ol><li><b>Face detection:</b> Find the face in an image or camera feed.</li><li><b>Face alignment:</b> Adjust the face using points such as eyes, nose and mouth.</li><li><b>Pre-processing:</b> Normalise image size, brightness and quality.</li><li><b>Feature extraction:</b> Create a numerical representation of facial features.</li><li><b>Template storage:</b> Store the enrolled face representation securely.</li><li><b>Matching:</b> Compare a new face with the saved template.</li><li><b>Decision:</b> If similarity passes the threshold, verify the person.</li></ol><div class="flow"><span>Camera image</span>→<span>Detect face</span>→<span>Extract features</span>→<span>Compare</span>→<span>Verify</span></div><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A phone scans its owner's face and compares it with the saved face template before unlocking.</div><p><b>Advantages:</b> Quick and convenient. <b>Limitations:</b> Lighting, camera quality, coverings and spoofing can affect results. Liveness checks can help.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Facial recognition is useful when facial data is protected and anti-spoofing checks are used.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 7</span><h3>Explain Bagging Techniques.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Bagging means Bootstrap Aggregating. It combines multiple models to improve stability and reduce prediction variation.</p><ol><li><b>Create samples:</b> Make different training samples by random sampling with replacement.</li><li><b>Train models:</b> Train a separate model on each sample.</li><li><b>Make predictions:</b> Each model predicts for new data.</li><li><b>Combine results:</b> Classification uses majority voting; regression uses averaging.</li><li><b>Final output:</b> Use the combined prediction.</li></ol><div class="flow"><span>Original data</span>→<span>Random samples</span>→<span>Many models</span>→<span>Vote / Average</span>→<span>Final result</span></div><h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Reduces variance and improves stability.</li><li>Works well with decision trees.</li><li>Models can often be trained in parallel.</li></ul><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> Random Forest combines predictions from many decision trees to classify a transaction.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Bagging combines several models to make predictions more stable.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 8</span><h3>Explain the steps involved in developing a basic fraud detection model using Machine Learning.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> A fraud detection model uses data and machine learning to identify transactions that may be fraudulent.</p><ol><li><b>Define problem:</b> Decide the fraud type to detect, such as card fraud.</li><li><b>Collect data:</b> Gather past transactions labelled genuine or fraud, where available.</li><li><b>Clean data:</b> Handle missing values, duplicates and incorrect entries.</li><li><b>Explore data:</b> Use tables and charts to understand amount, time and location patterns.</li><li><b>Prepare features:</b> Select useful inputs such as amount, time, location and transaction frequency.</li><li><b>Split data:</b> Separate training data and testing data.</li><li><b>Train model:</b> Use Logistic Regression, Decision Tree or Random Forest.</li><li><b>Evaluate:</b> Check precision, recall, F1-score and confusion matrix. Accuracy alone may mislead when fraud is rare.</li><li><b>Deploy and monitor:</b> Flag suspicious activity and update the model as fraud changes.</li></ol><div class="flow"><span>Collect</span>→<span>Clean</span>→<span>Prepare</span>→<span>Train</span>→<span>Test</span>→<span>Monitor</span></div><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A bank trains a Random Forest model on old card transactions. An unusual amount and location in a new transaction may cause an alert.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> A good model needs clean data, useful features, correct testing and regular monitoring.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 9</span><h3>Explain the role of IBM Watson in analysing data for fraud detection.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> IBM Watson refers to IBM's AI technologies and services. Depending on the product and setup, IBM AI tools can help analyse large amounts of data and support fraud detection.</p><ol><li><b>Data analysis:</b> Helps analyse large transaction and user-activity datasets.</li><li><b>Pattern identification:</b> AI analytics can identify unusual behaviour and patterns.</li><li><b>Risk prediction:</b> Models can estimate whether a transaction is suspicious.</li><li><b>Automation:</b> Can support alerts and investigation workflows when integrated with business systems.</li><li><b>Decision support:</b> Gives information to help analysts investigate suspicious cases.</li><li><b>Continuous improvement:</b> Models and rules can be reviewed as fraud patterns change.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A financial company uses IBM AI analytics in its monitoring system. A transaction that differs greatly from the customer's normal pattern is flagged for human review.</div><p><b>Important note:</b> IBM Watson is not one single fraud-detection button. Exact features depend on the IBM service, model and integration. Human review remains important.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> IBM AI tools can support faster analysis, pattern detection and investigation when correctly configured.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • 10</span><h3>Explain the role of Cloud AI solutions in fraud prevention.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Cloud AI uses artificial intelligence and machine-learning services hosted on cloud platforms to help detect and prevent fraud.</p><ol><li><b>Large-scale processing:</b> Processes large amounts of transaction data.</li><li><b>Real-time monitoring:</b> Analyses transactions quickly and flags suspicious activity.</li><li><b>Pattern detection:</b> Learns normal behaviour and finds unusual patterns.</li><li><b>Risk scoring:</b> Gives a risk score based on transaction features.</li><li><b>Scalability:</b> Cloud resources can grow when data or users increase.</li><li><b>Automatic alerts:</b> Sends alerts to security teams or customers.</li><li><b>Model updates:</b> Models can be retrained when fraud methods change.</li><li><b>Integration:</b> Can connect with banking, payment and security systems.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> An online payment company uses cloud AI to check payments. If a payment comes from an unusual device, location and amount, it may be flagged or require extra verification.</div><h4>Advantages and challenges</h4><ul><li>Fast analysis, scalability and less manual work.</li><li>Customer data needs encryption and access control.</li><li>Network availability, false alerts and model bias must be monitored.</li></ul><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Cloud AI helps detect suspicious activity at scale, but privacy, security and human oversight are necessary.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="photo-five" class="section"><h2>Extra 5-Mark Questions from Your Handwritten Question Paper</h2>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • EXTRA 1</span><h3>Explain the role of Data Mining.</h3><ol><li>Data mining finds useful patterns from large amounts of data.</li><li>It helps find unusual activities and possible cyber attacks.</li><li>It can group similar events and users.</li><li>It helps find trends in past security records.</li><li>It supports faster decisions by security teams.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> Data mining can study old network records and find repeated signs of a malware attack.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • EXTRA 2</span><h3>Explain optimisation in an AI algorithm.</h3><ol><li>Optimisation means improving the performance of an AI model.</li><li>Choose a suitable algorithm and settings.</li><li>Train the model using training data.</li><li>Measure errors and model performance.</li><li>Adjust settings to reduce errors and improve results.</li><li>Test the improved model using new data.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> Change the learning rate or tree depth to improve a fraud detection model.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • EXTRA 3</span><h3>Explain the working principle of Support Vector Machine (SVM).</h3><ol><li>SVM is a supervised machine-learning algorithm.</li><li>It learns from labelled examples.</li><li>It finds a decision boundary that separates two classes.</li><li>It tries to keep the largest possible gap, called the margin, between the classes.</li><li>It uses the boundary to classify new data.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> SVM learns from spam and normal emails, then classifies a new email as spam or not spam.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • EXTRA 4</span><h3>Explain applications of Deep Learning in malware detection.</h3><ol><li>Deep learning studies large amounts of file and network data.</li><li>It learns complex patterns linked to malware.</li><li>It can help identify unknown or changing malware patterns.</li><li>It can study file behaviour and network activity.</li><li>It can support automatic malware alerts and classification.</li><li>It needs good-quality data and enough computing power.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A neural-network model studies file behaviour and marks a file as suspicious when it shows harmful patterns.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • EXTRA 5</span><h3>Explain the working of a Decision Tree in malware detection.</h3><ol><li>Collect examples of safe and harmful files.</li><li>Find useful features such as file size, permissions and behaviour.</li><li>The tree asks questions about these features.</li><li>Each answer follows a branch to another question or result.</li><li>The final leaf gives a class such as safe or suspicious.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> If a file has unusual behaviour and requests risky permissions, the decision tree may classify it as suspicious.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • EXTRA 6</span><h3>Explain the role of Network Anomaly Detection Techniques.</h3><ol><li>They study normal network activity.</li><li>They find unusual traffic or behaviour.</li><li>They can detect sudden traffic increases.</li><li>They help identify possible malware, botnets and attacks.</li><li>They send alerts so security staff can investigate.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A computer suddenly sends a very large amount of traffic. The system flags it for checking.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">5 MARK • EXTRA 7</span><h3>Explain the effectiveness of ML algorithms in Botnet Detection.</h3><ol><li>ML learns patterns from normal and botnet traffic.</li><li>It checks network traffic automatically.</li><li>It can find unusual communication between devices.</li><li>It can classify traffic as normal or suspicious.</li><li>It can process large amounts of network data.</li><li>It needs good training data and regular updates.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A model detects many infected devices contacting the same suspicious server.</div></div>
</section>
<section id="photo-eight" class="section"><h2>Extra 8-Mark Questions from Your Handwritten Question Paper</h2>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 1</span><h3>Define AI and explain its objectives in Cyber Security.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Artificial Intelligence (AI) is a technology that helps computers learn, find patterns and make decisions. In cyber security, AI helps protect computers, networks and information.</p><h4>Objectives</h4><ol><li><b>Threat detection:</b> Find possible attacks, malware and suspicious activity.</li><li><b>Quick response:</b> Help security teams react faster to threats.</li><li><b>Network protection:</b> Watch network traffic and find unusual behaviour.</li><li><b>Fraud detection:</b> Identify unusual payments and account activity.</li><li><b>Spam and phishing detection:</b> Find harmful emails and fake messages.</li><li><b>Risk prediction:</b> Study patterns to estimate possible risks.</li><li><b>Reduce manual work:</b> Automate repeated security checks.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> AI can notice that a user account suddenly logs in from an unusual country and makes a large payment. It can raise an alert for checking.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> AI helps detect threats quickly and improve cyber security, but people must still check important decisions.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 2</span><h3>Explain the objectives and architecture of an Expert System.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> An Expert System is an AI program that uses knowledge and rules from human experts to solve a problem or give advice.</p><h4>Objectives</h4><ol><li>Give advice like a human expert in a specific area.</li><li>Provide quick and consistent decisions.</li><li>Store expert knowledge for future use.</li><li>Help users solve problems when an expert is not available.</li><li>Explain how a conclusion was reached.</li></ol><h4>Main parts of the architecture</h4><ol><li><b>Knowledge Base:</b> Stores facts and expert rules.</li><li><b>Inference Engine:</b> Uses the rules and facts to reach a conclusion.</li><li><b>User Interface:</b> Allows the user to enter questions and receive answers.</li><li><b>Working Memory:</b> Holds information about the current problem.</li><li><b>Explanation Facility:</b> Explains why the system gave a result.</li><li><b>Knowledge Acquisition:</b> Helps add or update expert knowledge.</li></ol><div class="flow"><span>User</span>↔<span>User Interface</span>↔<span>Inference Engine</span>↔<span>Knowledge Base</span></div><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A cyber security expert system can use stored rules to advise whether a network event looks suspicious.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> An expert system stores expert knowledge and uses rules to provide useful decisions in a specific field.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 3</span><h3>Analyse the evolution of AI from Expert Systems to Data Mining.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> AI has developed from rule-based systems that follow expert instructions to modern systems that learn patterns from large amounts of data.</p><ol><li><b>Expert Systems:</b> Early AI systems used rules written by human experts. They worked well in limited areas but could not easily learn new rules by themselves.</li><li><b>Machine Learning:</b> Systems began learning patterns from examples instead of depending only on fixed rules.</li><li><b>Data collection:</b> Computers and networks started producing large amounts of data.</li><li><b>Data Mining:</b> Tools were developed to find useful patterns, links and trends in large datasets.</li><li><b>Advanced AI:</b> Modern AI combines machine learning, data mining and deep learning to study complex information.</li><li><b>Cyber security use:</b> These methods help detect malware, fraud, unusual network traffic and phishing.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> An expert system may use a fixed rule such as “block this known harmful file”. A data-mining system can study many past files and discover new patterns that may indicate malware.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> AI has moved from fixed expert rules towards learning from data. Both rule-based and data-based methods can still be useful.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 4</span><h3>Illustrate the process of training a Machine Learning algorithm.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Training means teaching a machine-learning model to find patterns from example data so it can make predictions on new data.</p><ol><li><b>Define the problem:</b> Decide what the model must predict.</li><li><b>Collect data:</b> Gather useful examples related to the problem.</li><li><b>Prepare data:</b> Remove errors, handle missing values and convert data into a useful form.</li><li><b>Select features:</b> Choose the important information for learning.</li><li><b>Split data:</b> Divide it into training and testing parts.</li><li><b>Choose algorithm:</b> Select a suitable method such as Decision Tree or SVM.</li><li><b>Train model:</b> Give the training data to the algorithm so it can learn patterns.</li><li><b>Evaluate:</b> Test the model using data it has not seen before.</li><li><b>Improve and use:</b> Adjust settings if needed, then use and monitor the model.</li></ol><div class="flow"><span>Collect Data</span>→<span>Clean Data</span>→<span>Select Features</span>→<span>Split Data</span>→<span>Train</span>→<span>Test</span>→<span>Use Model</span></div><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> To detect malware, give the model examples of safe and harmful files. It learns their features and then checks a new file.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Good data, correct training and proper testing help build a useful ML model.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 5</span><h3>Compare Supervised, Unsupervised and Reinforcement Learning.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> These are three main types of machine learning. They differ in the way a computer learns.</p><h4>1. Supervised Learning</h4><ul><li>Uses labelled data, where the correct answer is already known.</li><li>Learns to predict an answer for new data.</li><li>Used for classification and prediction.</li><li>Example: Classifying emails as spam or not spam.</li></ul><h4>2. Unsupervised Learning</h4><ul><li>Uses data without given labels.</li><li>Finds groups, patterns or unusual records by itself.</li><li>Used for clustering and anomaly detection.</li><li>Example: Grouping similar network traffic and finding unusual groups.</li></ul><h4>3. Reinforcement Learning</h4><ul><li>Learns by taking actions and receiving rewards or penalties.</li><li>Tries to choose actions that give a better total reward.</li><li>Used where decisions happen step by step.</li><li>Example: A system learns a better response strategy through rewards in a safe training environment.</li></ul><h4>Difference summary</h4><p>Supervised = labelled examples; Unsupervised = no labels, find patterns; Reinforcement = learn from rewards and penalties.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Each type is useful for different tasks in AI and cyber security.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 6</span><h3>Explain the process of training a Machine Learning algorithm for Malware Detection.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> An ML malware detector learns the differences between safe and harmful files by studying examples.</p><ol><li><b>Collect samples:</b> Gather known safe and malware samples from trusted security datasets.</li><li><b>Label samples:</b> Mark them as safe or malware.</li><li><b>Extract features:</b> Collect details such as file size, file type, permissions and safe behaviour indicators.</li><li><b>Clean data:</b> Remove incorrect records and prepare the features.</li><li><b>Split dataset:</b> Make training and testing sets.</li><li><b>Choose algorithm:</b> Select Decision Tree, Random Forest, SVM or another suitable model.</li><li><b>Train:</b> Let the model learn from the labelled training examples.</li><li><b>Test:</b> Check how well it classifies unseen samples.</li><li><b>Deploy and update:</b> Use it to flag suspicious files and update it with new, verified samples.</li></ol><div class="flow"><span>Safe + Malware Samples</span>→<span>Label</span>→<span>Extract Features</span>→<span>Train Model</span>→<span>Test</span>→<span>Classify File</span></div><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A model learns from labelled safe and harmful files. When a new file has features similar to malware samples, it marks the file as suspicious for further checking.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Proper samples, useful features and regular updates help improve malware detection.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 7</span><h3>Demonstrate the working of Naive Bayes for classifying emails.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Naive Bayes is a machine-learning classification method based on Bayes' theorem. It uses the words in an email to estimate whether it is Spam or Ham (not spam).</p><h4>Working steps</h4><ol><li>Collect emails already labelled Spam or Ham.</li><li>Study the words that appear in each group.</li><li>Calculate how common each word is in Spam and Ham emails.</li><li>When a new email arrives, check its words.</li><li>Calculate the probability that the email is Spam and the probability that it is Ham.</li><li>Choose the class with the higher probability.</li></ol><p><b>Bayes' theorem:</b> P(Spam | Words) = P(Words | Spam) × P(Spam) / P(Words)</p><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A new email says “You won a prize, click this link”. If these words are common in the Spam examples, the model may give Spam a higher probability and place the email in Spam.</div><p><b>Advantages:</b> Simple, fast and useful for text classification. <b>Limitation:</b> It makes a simplifying assumption that features are independent, which is not always true.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Naive Bayes uses word probabilities to classify an email as Spam or Ham.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 8</span><h3>Illustrate the complete process of AI-based cyber threat detection from data collection to threat classification.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> AI-based threat detection uses data and machine learning to find and classify possible cyber attacks.</p><ol><li><b>Data collection:</b> Collect network traffic, system logs, login records and file activity.</li><li><b>Data cleaning:</b> Remove duplicate or incorrect records and handle missing values.</li><li><b>Feature extraction:</b> Select useful details such as IP address, traffic volume, login time and file behaviour.</li><li><b>Data preparation:</b> Convert the information into a format the AI model can use.</li><li><b>Model training:</b> Train the model using known normal and threat examples, when labels are available.</li><li><b>Threat detection:</b> Check new activity and find unusual patterns.</li><li><b>Threat classification:</b> Classify the activity, for example as normal, malware, phishing or possible intrusion.</li><li><b>Alert and response:</b> Send an alert to the security team, who can investigate and take action.</li><li><b>Model update:</b> Use verified new cases to improve future detection.</li></ol><div class="flow"><span>Collect Data</span>→<span>Clean</span>→<span>Extract Features</span>→<span>AI Model</span>→<span>Detect</span>→<span>Classify</span>→<span>Alert</span></div><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> The system collects network traffic and notices a device making many unusual connections. It classifies the activity as a possible botnet or attack and alerts the security team.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> AI helps examine data quickly and classify threats, while security staff confirm important alerts.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 9</span><h3>Explain the applications of Machine Learning algorithms.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> Machine Learning allows computers to learn patterns from data and make predictions or decisions.</p><ol><li><b>Spam detection:</b> Classifies emails as spam or normal.</li><li><b>Malware detection:</b> Finds files or behaviour that may be harmful.</li><li><b>Fraud detection:</b> Flags unusual bank or online payment transactions.</li><li><b>Network security:</b> Finds unusual network traffic and possible attacks.</li><li><b>Botnet detection:</b> Identifies suspicious communication between infected devices.</li><li><b>User authentication:</b> Helps check typing behaviour and account activity.</li><li><b>Phishing detection:</b> Finds suspicious links, messages and websites.</li><li><b>Threat prediction:</b> Studies past events to help estimate future risks.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A bank uses a machine-learning model to identify a payment that is very different from a customer's usual spending pattern.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> ML is useful in many cyber security tasks because it can learn patterns and examine large amounts of data.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 10</span><h3>Explain the architecture of a Botnet and its communication mechanism.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> A botnet is a group of infected computers or devices that an attacker controls. Each infected device is called a bot or zombie. The attacker is often called the botmaster.</p><h4>Main parts</h4><ol><li><b>Botmaster:</b> The attacker who controls the botnet.</li><li><b>Command-and-Control (C2):</b> The system or network used to send instructions.</li><li><b>Bots/Zombies:</b> Infected devices that receive and carry out commands.</li><li><b>Victim or target:</b> A system or service attacked by the botnet.</li></ol><h4>Communication types</h4><ul><li><b>Centralised:</b> Bots contact a central C2 server to receive instructions.</li><li><b>Peer-to-peer (P2P):</b> Bots share information with other bots, so there is no single central point for all communication.</li><li><b>Hybrid:</b> Combines centralised and P2P methods.</li></ul><div class="flow"><span>Botmaster</span>→<span>C2 System</span>→<span>Infected Bots</span>→<span>Target</span></div><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> An attacker controls many infected devices and orders them to send unwanted traffic to a website, making the website slow or unavailable.</div><h4>Effects and protection</h4><p>Botnets can be used for DDoS attacks, spam and data theft. Security tools, updates, network monitoring and removing malware help protect devices.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Understanding botnet parts and communication helps security teams detect and stop botnet activity.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><span class="tag">8 MARK • EXTRA 11</span><h3>Explain the importance of AI in detecting network-based cyber threats.</h3><p><b>Introduction:</b> AI helps security systems study network activity and identify possible attacks quickly.</p><ol><li><b>Fast monitoring:</b> Can examine large amounts of network traffic.</li><li><b>Anomaly detection:</b> Finds activity that is different from normal network behaviour.</li><li><b>Attack detection:</b> Helps identify scanning, unusual connections and possible intrusions.</li><li><b>Malware and botnet detection:</b> Learns patterns linked to infected devices.</li><li><b>Threat classification:</b> Helps group alerts into possible threat types.</li><li><b>Quick alerts:</b> Sends warnings so security teams can respond.</li><li><b>Learning from data:</b> Models can be updated using verified new attack examples.</li><li><b>Less manual work:</b> Automates repeated checks and helps analysts focus on serious events.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> AI notices that one computer is suddenly contacting many unknown devices. It flags the behaviour as suspicious for investigation.</div><p><b>Conclusion:</b> AI improves the speed and scale of network threat detection, but human checking is still needed to avoid mistakes.</p></div>
