<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>1. Analyse the role of strong passwords, account lockout and multi-factor authentication in preventing authentication abuse.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Authentication checks whether a person is the real owner of an account. Authentication abuse happens when an attacker tries to enter an account without permission. Strong passwords, account lockout and multi-factor authentication help protect accounts.</p>
<h4>1. Strong passwords</h4><ul><li>Use a long password with capital letters, small letters, numbers and symbols.</li><li>Avoid easy details such as names, birthdays and simple words.</li><li>Use a different password for each account.</li><li>Strong passwords make password guessing and brute-force attacks harder.</li></ul>
<h4>2. Account lockout</h4><ul><li>Account lockout temporarily blocks login after several wrong attempts.</li><li>It stops an attacker from trying unlimited passwords.</li><li>The system may lock the account for a short time and alert the owner or administrator.</li><li>It helps reduce brute-force attacks.</li></ul>
<h4>3. Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)</h4><ul><li>MFA checks a user with two or more different methods.</li><li>It may use a password, OTP, fingerprint or face scan.</li><li>Even if a password is stolen, the attacker still needs the extra check.</li></ul>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A student enters a strong password and then receives an OTP on their phone. A person who has only stolen the password cannot easily enter the account without the OTP.</div>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Strong passwords, account lockout and MFA work together to reduce unauthorised access and protect user accounts.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>2. Explain different types of authentication abuse and their effects.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Authentication abuse means using dishonest methods to enter another person's account without permission. Attackers use different methods to steal passwords or bypass login security.</p>
<h4>Types of authentication abuse</h4>
<ol><li><b>Brute-force attack:</b> The attacker tries many password combinations until the correct password is found.</li><li><b>Credential stuffing:</b> The attacker uses usernames and passwords stolen from one website to try to enter other websites.</li><li><b>Password spraying:</b> The attacker tries a small number of common passwords against many accounts.</li><li><b>Phishing:</b> The attacker sends a fake email, message or website link to trick a user into entering their password.</li><li><b>Session hijacking:</b> The attacker steals active session information and uses it to access an account without logging in normally.</li></ol>
<h4>Effects</h4><ol><li>Personal information may be stolen.</li><li>Money may be lost from bank or payment accounts.</li><li>Private messages and files may be accessed.</li><li>The stolen account may be used to send harmful messages.</li><li>Organisations may lose important information.</li><li>Users may lose trust in the service.</li><li>Accounts may be locked and services may be interrupted.</li></ol>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A user enters their bank password on a fake website. The attacker uses the stolen details to try to enter the real bank account and steal money.</div>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Authentication abuse can cause financial loss, privacy problems and data theft. Strong passwords, MFA, login limits and user awareness help prevent it.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>3. Explain Account Reputation Scoring.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Account Reputation Scoring checks whether a user account is trustworthy or suspicious. The system studies account details and activities and gives a score to show the level of risk.</p>
<h4>Factors considered</h4><ol><li><b>Login history:</b> Checks the usual login time and date.</li><li><b>Device information:</b> Checks whether the user is using a known or new device.</li><li><b>IP address:</b> Checks whether the network address is trusted or suspicious.</li><li><b>Location:</b> Checks whether the login location is unusual.</li><li><b>Failed login attempts:</b> Many wrong passwords may show an attack.</li><li><b>Account activity:</b> Checks unusual downloads, payments or account changes.</li><li><b>Previous security problems:</b> Checks whether suspicious activity happened before.</li></ol>
<h4>Working steps</h4><ol><li>Collect account login information.</li><li>Check the device and IP address.</li><li>Check login location and time.</li><li>Study the user's normal activities.</li><li>Compare new activity with the normal pattern.</li><li>Calculate the risk score.</li><li>Allow access, ask for extra verification or block access.</li><li>Send an alert if the activity is suspicious.</li></ol>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A student normally logs in from the same laptop in the evening. Suddenly, the account is accessed from a new device at midnight and many files are downloaded. The system may give the account a high-risk score and ask for extra verification.</div>
<h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Finds suspicious account activity.</li><li>Helps prevent account theft.</li><li>Supports safer login decisions.</li><li>Helps security teams investigate unusual actions.</li><li>Can reduce fraud and data theft.</li></ul>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Account Reputation Scoring protects accounts by studying login history, devices, locations and user behaviour before allowing access.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>4. Explain the steps involved in Keystroke Recognition.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Keystroke Recognition checks a person's identity by studying how they type on a keyboard. Each person may have a different typing speed, rhythm and way of pressing keys. It is also called Keystroke Dynamics.</p>
<h4>Steps involved</h4><ol><li><b>User registration:</b> The user types a given word, sentence or password several times.</li><li><b>Data collection:</b> The system records when each key is pressed and released.</li><li><b>Feature extraction:</b> The system finds typing features such as key holding time and time between keys.</li><li><b>Pattern creation:</b> The system creates a typing pattern for the user.</li><li><b>Pattern storage:</b> The pattern is stored safely as a reference.</li><li><b>New typing:</b> During a later login, the user types again.</li><li><b>Pattern comparison:</b> The new typing pattern is compared with the stored pattern.</li><li><b>Identity decision:</b> If the patterns are close enough, the user may be accepted. If they are very different, extra checking may be requested.</li></ol>
<h4>Important terms</h4><ul><li><b>Dwell time:</b> Time between pressing and releasing the same key.</li><li><b>Flight time:</b> Time between one key event and the next key event.</li></ul>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A user usually types a password with a steady speed and rhythm. If someone else steals the password but types it in a very different way, the system may notice and ask for extra verification.</div>
<h4>Advantages and limitations</h4><ul><li>No special device is needed.</li><li>It can work with a normal keyboard.</li><li>It adds another layer of account security.</li><li>Typing speed can change when a person is tired or stressed.</li><li>Different keyboards may affect typing.</li><li>The system needs enough typing samples.</li></ul>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Keystroke Recognition checks how a person types. It can support password login and help detect unauthorised users.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>5. Identify and explain the types of Keystroke Features.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Keystroke features are details collected when a person types on a keyboard. These details help a system learn and compare a user's typing style.</p>
<h4>Main types of keystroke features</h4><ol><li><b>Dwell time:</b> The time a key is held down. It starts when the key is pressed and ends when it is released.</li><li><b>Flight time:</b> The time between two key events. It shows how quickly a person moves from one key to another.</li><li><b>Typing speed:</b> Measures how quickly a person types a word or sentence.</li><li><b>Typing rhythm:</b> The regular pattern and timing of typing.</li><li><b>Key press time:</b> Records the time when a key is pressed.</li><li><b>Key release time:</b> Records the time when a key is released. It is used with press time to calculate dwell time.</li><li><b>Digraph time:</b> Measures the time taken between typing two keys one after another, such as A then B.</li><li><b>Error and correction pattern:</b> Studies typing mistakes and how the user corrects them, such as using Backspace.</li></ol>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> When a student types “SECURITY”, the system records key press time, key release time, time between keys and typing speed. These details form a typing pattern.</div>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Keystroke features describe a person's typing behaviour. They help compare typing patterns and support user authentication.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>6. Explain the steps involved in Facial Recognition.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Facial Recognition is a biometric method that identifies or checks a person by studying their face. It is used in smartphones, offices and security systems.</p>
<h4>Steps involved</h4><ol><li><b>Face capture:</b> A camera takes an image or video of the user's face.</li><li><b>Face detection:</b> The system finds the face in the image.</li><li><b>Face alignment:</b> The system adjusts the face image so important parts are in the correct position.</li><li><b>Feature extraction:</b> The system studies features such as the position of the eyes, nose, mouth and face shape.</li><li><b>Face template creation:</b> The features are changed into numerical information. This is called a face template.</li><li><b>Template storage:</b> During registration, the reference template is stored safely.</li><li><b>Face comparison:</b> During login, a new face template is created and compared with the stored template.</li><li><b>Final decision:</b> If the match is strong enough, access is allowed. Otherwise, access is rejected or another check is requested.</li></ol>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A user registers their face on a smartphone. Later, the camera scans the face and compares it with the saved template. The phone unlocks if the match is correct.</div>
<h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Easy to use.</li><li>No need to remember a password.</li><li>Fast identity checking.</li><li>Contactless method.</li><li>Useful for phones and office access systems.</li></ul>
<h4>Limitations</h4><ul><li>Poor lighting can affect the result.</li><li>Photos or videos may fool weak systems.</li><li>Face information must be protected.</li><li>Some systems may make incorrect matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Facial Recognition uses face features to verify identity. It provides a simple and fast method of biometric authentication.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>7. Explain the Bagging Technique.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Bagging means <b>Bootstrap Aggregating</b>. It is a machine learning method that combines the results of many models to make a better final prediction. It is commonly used to improve the stability of predictions.</p>
<h4>Steps involved</h4><ol><li><b>Collect data:</b> Prepare the original training dataset.</li><li><b>Create samples:</b> Create several new samples by randomly selecting records from the original data. Some records may appear more than once.</li><li><b>Train models:</b> Train a separate model using each sample.</li><li><b>Make predictions:</b> Each model makes its own prediction.</li><li><b>Combine results:</b> Collect the predictions from all models.</li><li><b>Majority voting:</b> For classification, select the answer chosen by most models.</li><li><b>Final result:</b> Give the combined answer as the final prediction.</li></ol>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A bank wants to decide whether a transaction is genuine or fraudulent. Five decision trees check it. Four trees say “Fraud” and one says “Genuine”. The final result is “Fraud” because most trees selected it.</div>
<h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Improves prediction stability.</li><li>Can reduce errors caused by changes in training data.</li><li>Can reduce overfitting in some models.</li><li>Combines the results of many models.</li><li>Useful for classification tasks.</li></ul>
<h4>Popular example – Random Forest</h4><p>Random Forest uses many decision trees. Each tree makes a prediction, and the system combines their answers using voting or averaging.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Bagging trains multiple models separately and combines their results. It can make predictions more stable and reliable.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>8. Explain the steps involved in developing a basic Fraud Detection Model using Machine Learning.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Fraud Detection means finding dishonest or unusual activities, such as stolen card payments or fake transactions. Machine Learning helps identify fraud by learning patterns from old transaction data.</p>
<h4>Steps involved</h4><ol><li><b>Define the problem:</b> Decide what type of fraud the system must detect, such as credit card fraud.</li><li><b>Collect data:</b> Collect transaction details such as amount, date, time, location and payment type.</li><li><b>Label the data:</b> If known, mark old transactions as genuine or fraudulent.</li><li><b>Clean the data:</b> Remove duplicate records and correct missing or incorrect values.</li><li><b>Select features:</b> Choose useful details such as transaction amount, location, time and number of recent transactions.</li><li><b>Split the data:</b> Divide the dataset into training data and testing data.</li><li><b>Choose an algorithm:</b> Select a suitable algorithm such as Decision Tree, Random Forest or Logistic Regression.</li><li><b>Train the model:</b> Give the training data to the algorithm so it can learn patterns.</li><li><b>Test the model:</b> Use testing data to check how well the model finds fraud.</li><li><b>Evaluate results:</b> Check correct detections, missed fraud and false alarms.</li><li><b>Use the model:</b> Connect the model to the transaction system to check new transactions.</li><li><b>Update the model:</b> Use new verified examples to improve future results.</li></ol>
<div class="diagram"><div class="node">Data Collection</div><div class="down">↓</div><div class="node">Data Cleaning</div><div class="down">↓</div><div class="node">Feature Selection</div><div class="down">↓</div><div class="node">Training</div><div class="down">↓</div><div class="node">Testing</div><div class="down">↓</div><div class="node">Fraud Prediction and Alert</div></div>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A bank collects old card transactions. It trains a Random Forest model using transactions marked genuine or fraudulent. When a new payment is made, the model checks its details and flags it if it looks suspicious.</div>
<h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Examines many transactions quickly.</li><li>Finds unusual spending patterns.</li><li>Supports faster alerts.</li><li>Reduces manual checking.</li><li>Can improve with good new data.</li></ul>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> A machine learning fraud model learns from old transactions and uses those patterns to identify suspicious new transactions.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>9. Explain the role of IBM Watson in analysing data for Fraud Detection.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> IBM Watson is a group of AI technologies and services developed by IBM. AI and data analysis tools can help organisations study large amounts of information and identify unusual patterns that may show fraud.</p>
<h4>Role in fraud detection</h4><ol><li><b>Data collection:</b> Work with information from transactions, customer records and other approved data sources.</li><li><b>Data processing:</b> Clean and prepare data for analysis.</li><li><b>Pattern finding:</b> AI tools help find unusual patterns in transactions.</li><li><b>Risk prediction:</b> Machine learning models estimate whether a transaction may be risky.</li><li><b>Real-time analysis:</b> Connected systems can analyse transactions as they happen, depending on the service and setup.</li><li><b>Alert creation:</b> Suspicious activity can be sent for review.</li><li><b>Data visualisation:</b> Results can be shown in reports, charts or dashboards using suitable data tools.</li><li><b>Decision support:</b> Results help bank staff decide whether to approve, review or stop a transaction.</li></ol>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> A bank studies a customer's normal card activity. A new payment is made from an unusual location for a much larger amount than normal. A fraud detection model may mark the payment as high risk and send it to the bank's security team.</div>
<h4>Benefits</h4><ul><li>Helps analyse large datasets.</li><li>Helps find unusual patterns.</li><li>Supports faster fraud checks.</li><li>Helps staff make data-based decisions.</li><li>Can connect with other data and security tools.</li></ul>
<div class="definition"><b>Important note:</b> IBM Watson is not one single fraud detection program. The exact features depend on the IBM product or service being used and how the organisation sets it up.</div>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> IBM Watson AI services can support data analysis and fraud detection by helping organisations find unusual patterns, estimate risk and send alerts for investigation.</p></div></article>
<article class="topic open"><div class="topic-head"><h3>10. Explain the role of Cloud AI Solutions in Fraud Prevention.</h3><span class="arrow">⌄</span></div><div class="topic-body"><p><b>Introduction:</b> Cloud AI Solutions use Artificial Intelligence and cloud computing to identify and prevent fraud. Cloud computing allows organisations to use computing power and storage over the internet instead of managing every system on their own computers.</p>
<h4>Role of Cloud AI in fraud prevention</h4><ol><li><b>Large data storage:</b> Cloud systems can store large amounts of transaction information.</li><li><b>Fast data processing:</b> Cloud computing can process large datasets quickly.</li><li><b>Pattern detection:</b> AI studies old transactions and learns normal customer behaviour.</li><li><b>Unusual activity detection:</b> It identifies transactions that are different from normal behaviour.</li><li><b>Risk scoring:</b> The system can give a risk score to each transaction.</li><li><b>Real-time alerts:</b> Connected systems can send alerts when suspicious transactions are detected.</li><li><b>Automatic action:</b> Based on the organisation's rules, a suspicious transaction may be paused or sent for extra verification.</li><li><b>Model updates:</b> AI models can be updated using new, verified fraud examples.</li><li><b>Scalability:</b> Cloud resources can be increased when transaction numbers grow.</li><li><b>Central monitoring:</b> Security teams can view alerts and reports from one system.</li></ol>
<div class="example"><b>Example:</b> An online shopping website receives thousands of payments. Cloud AI checks payment details such as amount, location and buying pattern. If a payment looks unusual, the system can flag it and ask for extra verification before completing it.</div>
<h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Handles large amounts of data.</li><li>Supports quick fraud checks.</li><li>Can be expanded when needed.</li><li>Reduces some manual work.</li><li>Helps organisations monitor transactions.</li><li>Supports updates to fraud detection models.</li></ul>
<h4>Challenges</h4><ul><li>Customer data must be protected.</li><li>Cloud services need a secure internet connection.</li><li>Incorrect alerts may affect genuine customers.</li><li>Organisations must manage access and privacy carefully.</li><li>Cloud services may have costs.</li></ul>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Cloud AI helps prevent fraud by storing and analysing large amounts of data, finding unusual transactions and sending alerts. Privacy and security must also be managed carefully.</p></div></article>
