<section id="u1" class="section"><h2>Unit 1 — Introduction to AI</h2>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Artificial Intelligence</h3><p>AI is a branch of computer science that makes computers perform tasks that normally need human intelligence, such as learning, problem-solving and decision-making.</p><ul><li>Detects unusual network activity.</li><li>Finds spam, phishing and malware.</li><li>Helps identify fraud.</li><li>Supports automatic security alerts.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Types of Machine Learning</h3><ol><li><b>Supervised:</b> Learns from labelled data, e.g. fraud/genuine transactions.</li><li><b>Unsupervised:</b> Finds patterns without labels, e.g. unusual network traffic.</li><li><b>Reinforcement:</b> Learns using rewards and penalties.</li></ol></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Expert Systems and Data Mining</h3><p><b>Expert System:</b> Uses stored expert rules to give decisions or advice. <b>Data Mining:</b> Finds useful patterns from large datasets.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Training and Optimisation</h3><ol><li>Collect and prepare data.</li><li>Choose an algorithm.</li><li>Train using training data.</li><li>Test performance.</li><li>Adjust settings to improve results.</li><li>Monitor after deployment.</li></ol></div></section>
<section id="u2" class="section"><h2>Unit 2 — Spam and Malware Detection</h2>
<div class="card topic"><h3>SVM for Spam Detection</h3><p>Support Vector Machine is a supervised algorithm. It finds a boundary that separates classes such as Spam and Not Spam (Ham). It learns from labelled email examples and classifies new emails using their features.</p><div class="example"><b>Example:</b> An email with suspicious links and repeated advertising words may be classified as spam.</div></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Naive Bayes</h3><p>Naive Bayes is a classification algorithm based on Bayes' theorem. It estimates the probability that an email belongs to a class.</p><p><b>Formula:</b> P(A|B) = [P(B|A) × P(A)] / P(B)</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Malware and its Types</h3><ul><li><b>Virus:</b> Attaches to files and spreads when they run.</li><li><b>Worm:</b> Spreads across systems or networks.</li><li><b>Trojan:</b> Pretends to be useful software but acts harmfully.</li><li><b>Ransomware:</b> Locks or encrypts data and demands payment.</li><li><b>Spyware:</b> Secretly collects information.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Decision Tree and Deep Learning for Malware</h3><p>A Decision Tree asks feature-based questions to classify a file or activity. Deep Learning uses neural networks with many layers to learn complex patterns from file, traffic or behaviour data. Deep learning needs good data and computing resources, and its decisions may be harder to explain.</p></div></section>
<section id="u3" class="section"><h2>Unit 3 — Network Security and Authentication Abuse</h2>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Network Anomaly Detection</h3><ol><li>Collect network traffic.</li><li>Study normal patterns.</li><li>Extract packet, connection and traffic-volume features.</li><li>Use rules or ML to detect unusual activity.</li><li>Send alerts for investigation.</li></ol></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Botnet Topologies</h3><ul><li><b>Centralised:</b> Bots receive commands from one central server.</li><li><b>P2P:</b> Bots communicate with one another.</li><li><b>Hybrid:</b> Combines both approaches.</li></ul><p>A botnet is a group of infected devices controlled by an attacker called a botmaster.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>ML Algorithms for Botnet Detection</h3><p>Decision Tree, Random Forest, SVM, Naive Bayes, K-Means and Neural Networks can study network traffic features and help identify botnet activity.</p></div></section>
<section id="u4" class="section"><h2>Unit 4 — Account Reputation and Biometrics</h2>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Biometric Authentication</h3><p>Physical biometrics include fingerprint, face and iris. Behavioural biometrics include typing pattern, voice behaviour and signature dynamics. Biometric data must be protected from theft and spoofing.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Keystroke Timing</h3><p><b>Dwell time:</b> How long a key stays pressed. <b>Flight time:</b> Time interval between key events, commonly releasing one key and pressing the next.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Facial Recognition and Liveness</h3><p>Facial recognition detects a face, extracts features and compares them with a saved template. Examples of approaches include Viola–Jones, MTCNN, FaceNet and ArcFace. Liveness detection checks whether the face is from a real present person rather than a photo or video.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>FAR, FRR and EER</h3><ul><li><b>FAR:</b> Wrong person is incorrectly accepted.</li><li><b>FRR:</b> Correct person is incorrectly rejected.</li><li><b>EER:</b> Point where FAR and FRR are equal.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Continuous Authentication</h3><p>Checks identity repeatedly during a session using signals such as typing or device behaviour. If risk increases, the system can ask for extra verification.</p></div></section>
<section id="u5" class="section"><h2>Unit 5 — AI for Fraud Prevention</h2>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Common ML Algorithms</h3><ul><li><b>Logistic Regression:</b> Estimates fraud probability.</li><li><b>Decision Tree:</b> Uses feature-based questions.</li><li><b>Random Forest:</b> Combines many trees.</li><li><b>SVM:</b> Separates classes.</li><li><b>Gradient Boosting / XGBoost:</b> Learns sequentially to reduce earlier errors.</li><li><b>Isolation Forest:</b> Finds unusual data points.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Bagging vs Boosting</h3><p><b>Bagging:</b> Trains models on different random samples, often in parallel, and combines by voting or averaging. Example: Random Forest.</p><p><b>Boosting:</b> Trains models one after another; each new model focuses on earlier errors. Examples: AdaBoost and Gradient Boosting.</p></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Jupyter Notebook and Python Libraries</h3><ul><li><b>Jupyter Notebook:</b> Interactive place to write and run Python code.</li><li><b>Pandas:</b> Manages table data.</li><li><b>NumPy:</b> Numerical arrays and calculations.</li><li><b>Matplotlib:</b> Charts and graphs.</li><li><b>Scikit-learn:</b> ML algorithms and evaluation tools.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card topic"><h3>Fraud Model Evaluation</h3><p><b>Precision:</b> Of predicted fraud cases, how many were actually fraud? <b>Recall:</b> Of all actual fraud cases, how many did the model find? <b>F1-score:</b> Combined measure of precision and recall. Fraud datasets are often imbalanced, so accuracy alone may be misleading.</p></div></section>
